WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.000
What would you do for the power to fly?

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.600
How about shedding your skin

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.830
and dissolving your own muscles?

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.570
Now, believe it or not,
that gruesome process

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.550
is how caterpillars earn their wings.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:14.910
Here's what you might not know

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.630
about what's inside a
caterpillar's "cocoon."

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.670
Contrary to popular belief,
this is not a cocoon.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.050
Only certain moths build cocoons,

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.060
which are like a silky sleeping
bag that covers the insect.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.500
This, on the other hand, is
what's called a chrysalis.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.600
It's not a sack or a pouch;

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.980
it's actually the caterpillar's own body.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.700
When it's time for the
transformation to begin,

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.020
the caterpillar's body ramps up production

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.150
of a hormone called ecdysone,

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.810
and that causes it to cast
off its outer coating,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.120
sort of like how a snake sheds its skin.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.705
And underneath is a hard shell

00:00:54.705 --> 00:00:57.830
similar to the exoskeleton of a beetle.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:02.210
After that, life for the
little caterpillar gets oozy.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.730
First, it releases
enzymes called caspases.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:09.900
These rip apart and dissolve
cells in its muscles,

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.940
digestive system, and other organs.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.940
But the enzymes don't quite
liquefy all of the caterpillar.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.780
They leave key structures
intact, like breathing tubes.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.170
At the same time, specialized cells

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.440
called imaginal discs start waking up.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.780
Before the chrysalis stage,
these discs were kept dormant

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.940
by a series of hormones
in the caterpillar's body.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.150
But once the transformation begins,

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.170
those hormone levels take a nosedive,

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:42.360
giving these discs the opportunity
to do what they do best:

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.300
build a butterfly.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.360
You see, each disc
contains the genetic recipe

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.990
to form a different adult body part,

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.290
starting from the inside out.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.480
After one week, the digestive system

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.180
of the butterfly is well on its way.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.800
And by day 16, the
adult's legs, wings, eyes,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.170
and mouth are all present
and in working order.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.030
Now, two weeks is a remarkably short time

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.848
for all of this to happen,

00:02:08.848 --> 00:02:12.850
since each imaginal disc starts
out with only about 50 cells

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:16.183
and must multiply those into thousands

00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:19.106
just to form a single wing.

00:02:19.106 --> 00:02:21.830
And if you checked out the
chrysalis around day 16,

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.500
you might even be able to see

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.283
those brilliantly colored wings.

00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:27.724
Because for some species,

00:02:27.724 --> 00:02:29.950
their chrysalis turns transparent

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.820
in their final days of metamorphosis.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.110
Now, fully formed, it's
time to hit the road.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.430
The chrysalis splits open down the center,

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.440
and the butterfly escapes.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.170
Meanwhile, a reddish liquid spills out.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.750
That's all the waste the butterfly,

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:49.350
née caterpillar, produced during its stay.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.740
Once its wings expand and
harden, it's ready to mate,

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.610
pollinate, and slurp nectar
to its heart's desire.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.550
But one of the most
interesting parts of all?

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.860
Research suggests that butterflies

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.290
and moths can remember
their caterpillar days.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:08.210
In one study, researchers
trained moth caterpillars

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.710
to associate an odor
with an electric shock,

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.020
so whenever the larvae
smelled it, they'd move away.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.840
But even after they
transformed into adult moths,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.020
they still avoided the scary smell.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.340
It makes you wonder what else

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.498
they could recall from their younger days.

